  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An anomalously strong upper level trough\namplifies over the eastern US today, with a potent shortwave \ndiving southeast through VA and the Carolinas. A strong LLJ \nahead of this feature should lead to increasing moisture \nadvection, with PWATs once again climbing back above 2\". \nMeanwhile, heating of a moist boundary layer should support \nmoderate instability, with MLCAPE rising into the 1500-2500 j/kg\nrange. MHX 18Z sounding shows MUCAPE in excess of 5kJ/kg, but\nwe've been relatively clear from the debris clouds . Aloft, a \nnotable upper jet max is forecast to overspread the instability \naxis, supporting deep layer shear of 30-45kt. While instability \nwill be moderate, the shear/instability/forcing combo is notable\nfor this time of year. This combination supports the threat for\nsevere weather, with modest support from ensemble, analog, and \nmachine learning guidance. The main potential limiting factors \nare timing and potential subsidence, with area remaining capped \nthrough much of today. Most guidance continues to show that the\nstronger convection will not reach ENC until, or after, sunset.\nDespite the potential for a gradually stabilizing boundary \nlayer, if strong large-scale forcing is realized, it should help\nsupport deep convection well past the typical diurnal max in \nconvective intensity this time of year. Storm mode could be a \nbit messy, with combination of supercells early ahead of more \norganized multicell line segments later. Given the potential \namount of forcing present, it appears likely that a MCS will \nmove through the eastern Carolinas and VA during the evening and\nearly overnight hours posing a risk of damaging winds. Low- \nlevel SRH around 100-200m2/s2 may support a few rotating \nupdrafts capable of hail and tornados. However, those latter 2 \nhazards should be lower compared to the wind risk. Given the \ntrend in the track of the low further away from the FA and the \nlater arrival of convection, SPC has removed the FA from the 5% \ntor threat, limiting the potential to 2% across all of ENC. \nWhile convection looks to be fairly progressive, heavy rain in a\nhigh PWAT airmass could pose a localized flood threat, esp for \nareas that have received heavy rain over the last few days (Hyde\ncounty east through the Outer Banks), where WPC is suggesting\n6hr rainfall amounts in excess of one inch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cold front will push offshore early Wed \nmorning, with sfc high pressure building in from the west while \ntroughing continues aloft. This will keep temps seasonable and \nmostly dry through late week. The next frontal system will \nimpact the area this weekend into early next week, with \nincreasing precip chances.",
